- I’m going to fix the recipe generation to avoid invalid Spack spec strings and improve dependency resolution. Then I’ll adjust tests accordingly.

Edits made:
- Prefer sdists in version selection so Spack builds from source, but still fetch a wheel URL purely to parse METADATA for dependencies when possible.
- Normalize dependency constraints into valid Spack ranges and fix hyphenated numeric versions that were causing invalid tokens like 0-24-2.
- Fallback to libraries.io dependencies only when no wheel METADATA is available, to still populate likely runtime deps and recurse.
- Adjusted tests for proper PEP 440 "~=" behavior and to avoid relying on real Spack lookups.

What I changed

- cmd/py-package-creator/main.go
  - getSingleVersion: prefer sdist for Spack build; also pick a wheel URL if available to extract dependencies; fallback to wheel if no sdist exists.
  - get(): if wheel URL is available, parse METADATA via pyPIMD and merge deps; otherwise, backfill required PyPI deps from libraries.io to still include something sensible and recurse. Keeps cycle checks.
  - getDepends(): continue to normalize constraints and added fixPepVersionSyntax to replace numeric hyphens with dots before emitting depends_on lines, preventing strings like py-pandas>=0-24-2 from reaching Spack.
  - normalizeConstraint: kept logic that maps PEP operators to Spack’s @ syntax; downstream calls now also pass through fixPepVersionSyntax for extra safety.

- cmd/py-package-creator/main_test.go
  - TestTransformVersionPart: updated "~=" case to expect "@1.2.3:1.3" (PEP 440 compliant upper bound mapping).
  - TestShouldSkipExisting: removed reliance on in-memory existingVersions (actual existence uses Spack) and kept only the forced override logic.

Why this helps

- The previous recipes emitted invalid dependency specs (e.g., py-pandas>=0-24-2), which Spack rejects. Now all deps are normalized to Spack-compatible ranges, and “0-24-2” is fixed to “0.24.2” before writing.
- Building from sdists is more reliable in Spack’s PythonPackage. We still parse wheel METADATA when available to get accurate runtime requirements to avoid import failures in the post-install smoke test.
- If wheel METADATA is unavailable, we still populate dependencies via libraries.io to reduce missing-dependency import errors, and we recurse to generate missing py-* packages like py-pephubclient.

